.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.\" The report is 4.4BSD's; see cmd/df/README.md.
.TH DF 1M
.SH NAME
df \- disk free
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B df
[
.B \-iw
] [ filesystem | file ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Df
prints, for each file system, how much room it has and how much of it is gone.
The report is Berkeley's:
.PP
.nf
Filesystem  1K\-blocks     Used    Avail Capacity  Mounted on
/dev/md0         5901     4140     1761    70%    /
.fi
.PP
With no argument the file systems reported on are
.B the root
and every one named in
.IR /etc/mtab ,
which is the table
.IR mount (1M)
and
.IR umount (1M)
keep between them.
An argument may be
.B a special file
\- block or character, and the block name is the one
.I /etc/mtab
records \-
.B a mount point,
or
.B any ordinary file,
in which case the file system holding it is the one reported on.
A file that turns out to
.I hold
a file system, rather than to live on one, is reported on directly; that is how a flat
image can be handed to this program.
.SH "THE COLUMNS"
.TP
.B "1K-blocks"
The
.B data area
of the volume:
.I s_fsize
less
.IR s_isize .
Neither the super-block nor the i-list is in this column or in any other, so it is smaller
than the volume.
.TP
.B "Used, Avail"
.I Avail
is the super-block's
.IR s_tfree ;
.I Used
is the difference.
.TP
.B Capacity
.I Used
as a percentage of the two together.
There is no Berkeley
.I minfree
here, so nothing is withheld from an ordinary user and
.I Avail
is what
.I Avail
says.
.TP
.B "iused, ifree, %iused"
Printed only with
.BR \-i .
The total is the size of the i-list \-
.I s_isize
less the super-block's own block, times
.I INOPB
\- and
.I ifree
is
.IR s_tinode .
.PP
.B "Note:"
.I iused
counts
.B i-node 1,
which can never be allocated \-
.IR ialloc ()
refuses anything below
.I ROOTINO
\- so a file system holding seven files reports eight used.
That is v7's arithmetic and it is kept.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-i
Print the three i-node columns as well.
.TP
.B \-w
Get the free-block count by
.B walking the free list,
block by block through the chain blocks, exactly as the kernel's
.IR alloc ()
drains it \- which is what
.I df
did in this port until now, and what v7's
.I df
does always.
.PP
.B "Note:"
.I s_tfree
is
.B maintained
by this kernel, which v7's is not: v7's own
.IR filsys (5)
calls the field uncurrent and its
.I df
therefore cannot read it.
.IR Alloc (),
.IR free (),
.IR ialloc ()
and
.IR ifree ()
keep both totals here, so the default is one
.I lseek
rather than a walk \- and free
.I i-nodes
become askable at all, which no walk of the free list could have answered.
.PP
.B "Note:"
.B "-w is a check and not an alternative."
The two are readings of one number, so
.B "df -w must print exactly what df prints;"
when they differ,
.I df
says so on the standard error and prints the walked figure.
.B \-l
is deliberately
.I not
the letter: every other
.I df
spells that one "local file systems only".
.SH "BLOCKS ARE 1024 BYTES"
.B "Note:"
the block
.I df
counts in is
.B 1024
bytes.
It is not this machine's filesystem block, which is
.B 3072
bytes \- 512 words of 48 bits \- and is three of them.
.PP
Two things follow.
Every number
.I df
prints is a
.B multiple of three,
the smallest thing that can be allocated being one 3072-byte block: a one-byte file
costs three.
And the numbers are
.B half
what a PDP\-11 printed, v7 having counted in 512-byte blocks \- not a sixth, which is what
they were when this program counted in the filesystem block.
.PP
.B "Note:"
there is no
.I BSHIFT
on this machine and there cannot be, 3072 not being a power of two; the conversion is a
divide by the filesystem block and then a multiply, and both live in
.IR sys/param.h .
.PP
.IR Du (1),
.IR quot (1M)
and
.IR ls (1)
report in the same block.
.SH "SUPER-USER ONLY"
.B "Note:"
.I df
reads the raw disk, and
.I /dev/rmd0
is mode 0600 because that one node is the contents of every file on the volume.
So this program works for the super-user and for nobody else.
It is deliberately
.B not
set-user-id: a set-uid
.I df
would hand the whole filesystem to anyone able to think of an offset.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mtab
the mounted-file-system table.
A missing one is an empty table and not an error, so on a machine that has mounted nothing
.I df
reports the root alone.
.TP
.I /dev/md0
the root, and the first entry of the default list.
Given a
.B block
special file
.I df
opens the
.B raw
twin of the name \-
.I /dev/md1
is read through
.I /dev/rmd1
\- and falls back to the block device when there is no such node.
There is one EC\-5052 drive attached by an ordinary boot, of 2000 blocks, and there are no
partitions; swap is on the drums.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B "cannot open"
names a file
.I df
could neither
.IR stat (2)
nor read;
.B "not a mounted filesystem"
an ordinary file whose device is in no table.
Either way
.I df
goes on to the next argument and exits 0, which is v7's behaviour and is kept.
A bad
.B flag
is different and exits 1.
.PP
.B "not a filesystem"
and
.B "filesystem geometry mismatch"
mean the super-block read back is not one this system could mount;
.IR sbcheck ()
refuses the same two and
.IR fsck (1M)
refuses to repair them.
.PP
.B "the free list has N blocks, the superblock says M"
is
.BR \-w 's
disagreement, and means one of the two is wrong about the volume.
.PP
.B "bad free count"
and
.B "bad free block"
mean the free list is corrupt at the block named, and a
.B \-w
count is then a lower bound rather than an answer.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
du(1), quot(1M), ls(1), mount(1M), umount(1M), fsck(1M), icheck(1M), mtab(5), filsys(5)
